WEBVTT

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:27.370
<v Elna Baker>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Elna Baker, sitting in for Ira Glass. Kids have a stage where they learn to be embarrassed. It begins when they're toddlers, but some of us don't seem to take the lesson to heart, like my friend Jane Marie.
<v Elna Baker>这里是芝加哥WBEZ电台的《美国生活》。我是艾尔娜·贝克，代班伊拉·格拉斯。孩子们在成长过程中会经历一个学习“尴尬”的阶段。这始于他们蹒跚学步时，但我们中的一些人似乎并没有把这门课放在心上，比如我的朋友简·玛丽。

00:00:27.370 --> 00:00:46.290
<v Elna Baker>I've known Jane for years. She used to work at the radio show. And her shamelessness was the quality I most admired about her, especially as a woman. You know, she didn't care what people thought. Am I acting nice? Am I dressed too sexy? Am I being loud? She didn't ask those questions. She was totally opinionated and scrappy.
<v Elna Baker>我认识简很多年了。她曾在电台工作。她的厚脸皮是我最钦佩她的品质，尤其对于女性而言。你知道，她不在乎别人怎么想。我表现得够好吗？我穿得太性感了吗？我说话声音太大吗？她从不问这些问题。她非常有主见，也很有拼劲。

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:59.940
<v Elna Baker>Jane was never embarrassed, and for a pretty specific reason. The way she grew up-- she was Methodist-- she was taught you are not the center of the universe, which made her feel free, like no one was watching.
<v Elna Baker>简从不感到尴尬，这有个相当具体的原因。她的成长经历——她信奉卫理公会——教会她：你并不是宇宙的中心，这让她感到自由，仿佛没人注视着她。

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:31.350
<v Jane Marie>To be embarrassed would be to assume that anyone in this room or anyone who witnessed this actually spends more than 5 seconds thinking about me. And they don't. Why would you think anyone but God gives a shit about anything you're doing, ever? They don't. Like, you are nothing. You mean nothing in the world. It's obnoxious, basically, to think that, like, any of your actions-- that anyone is actually looking in your direction.
<v Jane Marie>感到尴尬就意味着假设在这个房间里的任何人，或者任何见证这一切的人，真的会花超过5秒钟的时间来关注我。而事实并非如此。你凭什么认为除了上帝之外，会有人在意你做的任何事，哪怕是一丁点？他们根本不在意。就像，你什么都不是。你在世界上毫无意义。认为自己的任何行为会被人盯着看，这简直是自作多情。

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:38.860
<v Elna Baker>This worldview, that others weren't judging her, helped her not judge herself. Those thoughts just didn't occur to her.
<v Elna Baker>这种世界观——即别人并没有在评判她——帮助她不去评判自己。那些念头根本就不会出现在她脑海里。

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:42.000
<v Elna Baker>Did you have an inner critic?
<v Elna Baker>你内心有批判的声音吗？

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.370
<v Jane Marie>What do you mean by inner critic?
<v Jane Marie>你指的内心批判是什么意思？

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.840
<v Elna Baker>Like, a voice that--
<v Elna Baker>就像，有一个声音会——

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:53.140
<v Jane Marie>(LAUGHING) That's actually funny, sorry. I'm like, [KNOCK KNOCK], come again?
<v Jane Marie>（笑）这真的很有趣，抱歉。我在想，[敲敲门]，你说什么？

00:01:53.140 --> 00:02:10.130
<v Elna Baker>So I was surprised when she texted me and said she'd heard I was looking for embarrassing stories to put on today's radio show. And that she had one, a big one that she'd never told anyone but her therapist, something that changed who she was. And I'll get to that story in about a minute.
<v Elna Baker>所以当她给我发短信说听说我正在为今天的节目寻找尴尬故事时，我非常惊讶。她说她有一个，一个重大的故事，除了她的治疗师，她从未告诉过任何人，这件事改变了她。我一分钟后就会讲到那个故事。

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:29.410
<v Elna Baker>But first, let me just say, I love embarrassing stories. I always ask for them at parties. If I hear a good one, I'll retell it for years. Some of them, so funny. But that's not the only reason I like them. I think the best ones say so much about us, reveal sides of ourselves maybe we'd rather not see.
<v Elna Baker>但首先，我得说，我喜欢尴尬的故事。我总是在派对上讨要这些故事。如果听到一个好的，我会复述好几年。有些确实非常搞笑。但这并不是我喜欢它们的唯一原因。我认为最好的故事能体现我们的很多特质，揭示出我们内心深处可能不愿面对的一面。

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:44.920
<v Elna Baker>And to prove the point that embarrassing stories have a lot to teach us, I decided to harness the power of the radio program. And we asked you for your embarrassing stories. People sent in over a thousand of them. Today we'll hear some of those and talk about what they reveal.
<v Elna Baker>为了证明尴尬故事能教给我们很多东西，我决定利用电台节目的影响力。我们向你们征集了尴尬故事。人们寄来了超过一千个。今天我们将聆听其中的一些，并讨论它们揭示了什么。

00:02:44.920 --> 00:03:01.960
<v Elna Baker>But we're going to kick things off with Jane's story because, honestly, of the hundreds of embarrassing stories that I've heard, I've never heard another one like Jane's, where it changed someone so deeply. Her story is our first act. Let's call it Belle of the Gall.
<v Elna Baker>但我们先从简的故事开始，因为老实说，在我听过的几百个尴尬故事里，我从没听过像简这样能让人发生如此深刻改变的故事。她的故事是我们节目的第一幕，我们称之为“尴尬女王”。

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:18.430
<v Elna Baker>Jane's story starts at a big charity event at a bougie hotel in Beverly Hills. The night is a fundraiser for children with epilepsy. It's held in a grand ballroom with fine china and excessive centerpieces and some of the most powerful producers and executives in Hollywood.
<v Elna Baker>简的故事开始于比弗利山庄一家高档酒店举行的大型慈善活动。那天晚上是为癫痫患儿举办的筹款晚会。现场布置在豪华宴会厅，用着精致的瓷器和华丽的餐桌花艺，座上宾包括好莱坞最有权势的制作人和高管。

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:28.840
<v Elna Baker>This is not Jane's scene. She's there because she's interviewing for a job, a job with a big studio. A job that would be a major leap for her and pay a ton more money than she'd ever made.
<v Elna Baker>这不属于简的世界。她出现在那儿是因为她在面试一份工作，一份大制片厂的工作。这份工作对她来说是一次重大飞跃，薪水远超她以往任何时候。

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:52.350
<v Elna Baker>They'd met with her several times for interviews, and they'd invited her tonight and paid for her ticket as a kind of fun, looser, let's go do something more social and see how we all hit it off. Knowing where it's held and knowing it's a gala, Jane wears a ball gown, a two-piece dark green dress that shows off her midriff with a giant billowy skirt. She walks into the event and, to her surprise--
<v Elna Baker>他们已经和她面试过好几次，邀请她今晚参加并支付了入场券，作为一种更轻松、更社交的方式，看看大家是否合拍。知道地点是晚宴，简穿了一件礼服，那是一件露出腰部的深绿色两件套裙装，裙摆宽大。她走进会场，惊讶地发现——

00:03:52.350 --> 00:04:11.350
<v Jane Marie>People were wearing, like, business casual instead of, like-- it wasn't black tie. And so I'm overdressed. They're passing around champagne flutes. I grab one but promise myself I'm only going to have half of this one glass of champagne before we sit down at the table. Like, I am going into this with full control of myself. Like, I am--
<v Jane Marie>大家穿的都是商务休闲装，而不是——那不是黑领结晚宴。所以我穿得太隆重了。他们在那儿传着香槟。我拿了一杯，但答应自己坐下前只喝半杯。我本想完全控制住局面的，我想——

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:13.090
<v Elna Baker>You're there to make a good impression.
<v Elna Baker>你去那里是为了给人留下好印象。

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:39.060
<v Jane Marie>I am there to make a great impression on these people. I want to work with them. And I want them to think I'm respectable. And it's a weird thing. Like, I hate that I still have to talk about the world this way. But when you're the only woman at the table, or one of two women at a table, and they're all, like, old, older white dudes, CEO types who, in a certain way, have your future in their hands. It doesn't feel great.
<v Jane Marie>我是去给这些人留下深刻印象的。我想和他们共事。我想让他们觉得我很体面。这很奇怪。我讨厌自己仍得这样谈论这个世界。但当你是一桌人里唯一的女性，或者唯二的女性之一，而他们全都是年长的白人男性、CEO类型，且在某种程度上掌握着你的未来。那种感觉并不好。

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:59.410
<v Elna Baker>In the ballroom of maybe 500 guests, they're seated at the main table, front and center. During dinner, Jane's looking for her opportunity to shine, a moment when they'll get to know her better. But it's not coming. The CEO guys are all talking over her. There's no moment to jump into the conversation. No one seems curious about her at all.
<v Elna Baker>在大概有500位客人的宴会厅里，他们坐在最中心的席位。晚宴期间，简一直在寻找闪光的机会，一个能让他们更了解她的时刻。但机会一直没来。CEO们一直在抢话。根本没有机会插话。似乎没人对她表现出好奇。

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:12.670
<v Jane Marie>So they get to the auction portion, or the fundraiser portion, of the evening. And there's a person up onstage who's calling the auction. And they say, all right, let's get things kicked off here.
<v Jane Marie>接着来到了拍卖环节，或者说是筹款环节。台上的人正在主持拍卖。他们说，好的，让我们开始吧。

00:05:12.670 --> 00:05:33.400
<v Elna Baker>Jane has an idea. One time-- the only other time she's been to a gala-- they'd had an auction too. And to surprise her table, as a joke, when they started the bidding, before any of the celebrities that night-- Lady Gaga or Angela Bassett or Heidi Klum-- could get in a bid, Jane raised her paddle.
<v Elna Baker>简有个主意。有一次——她唯一去过的另一次晚宴——也有拍卖环节。为了给同桌制造惊喜，开玩笑地，当竞拍刚开始，在所有明星——Lady Gaga、安吉拉·贝塞特或海蒂·克鲁姆——出价之前，简举起了牌子。

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:55.750
<v Elna Baker>It was for an item that was tens of thousands of dollars. Jane was broke. Her friends at the table knew she couldn't afford it. But she figured, I'm surrounded by rich people. Someone will absolutely outbid me. And they did. The item went for hundreds of thousands of dollars. Everyone thought it was hilarious. So she looked at this table of CEOs and thinks--
<v Elna Baker>那是件价值几万美元的拍品。简当时身无分文。同桌的朋友知道她买不起。但她心想，周围全是富人，肯定有人会出价更高。结果确实如此。那件拍品最终拍出了几十万美元。大家都觉得这很有趣。于是她看着这桌CEO，心想——

00:05:55.750 --> 00:06:20.140
<v Jane Marie>I know how to spice up the evening because I've done it before. I'm going to throw my paddle up again. I'm going to throw my paddle up again, get things cranking, have a funny story for our table to laugh about. And so they opened the bidding. And what I hear is, we're going to start the bidding out at $25,000.
<v Jane Marie>我知道怎么炒热气氛，因为我以前干过。我要再次举牌。我要再次举牌，让气氛活跃起来，好让我们这桌有个笑话聊。于是他们开启了竞拍。我听到的是，起拍价是25000美元。

00:06:20.140 --> 00:06:31.260
<v Jane Marie>And I shoot my paddle up in the air with the same confidence that it'll get outbid immediately and then we're moving on. And then we're cooking with gas, and everybody's like, you're a blast, you know.
<v Jane Marie>于是我带着同样的自信将牌子举到半空，心想马上就会有人出价更高，然后我们就能继续了。这样大家就会觉得我很风趣，你知道。

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:35.890
<v Elna Baker>Jane feels a rush. Ha, that was fun. She waits to be outbid.
<v Elna Baker>简感到一阵兴奋。哈哈，这真有趣。她等着别人出更高价。

00:06:35.890 --> 00:06:48.870
<v Jane Marie>And I had somehow not heard that this round of bidding was really just-- it wasn't something that anyone else could bid on.
<v Jane Marie>而我当时完全没听出来，这一轮竞拍并不是——这不是其他人能出价的拍品。

00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:52.420
<v Elna Baker>It was not an auction at all. It was a pledge.
<v Elna Baker>这根本不是拍卖，这是一个认捐环节。

00:06:52.420 --> 00:07:23.160
<v Jane Marie>So it was a $25,000 pledge from the eight people sitting at my table. It all happened really quickly. After I raised the paddle, they were like, yay. And they put the name of our table and everyone at our table up on this board. There was a TV screen, a large TV screen hanging from the ceiling, and the name of the company I was with and our table number and the amount of my pledge.
<v Jane Marie>所以这是那一桌八个人要认捐25000美元。一切发生得太快了。我举牌后，他们欢呼起来。他们把我们桌的名号和每个人的名字都投到了大屏幕上。那是悬挂在天花板上的大屏幕，上面写着我所在公司的名称、我们的桌号以及我的认捐金额。

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:56.250
<v Jane Marie>And I look over, and the one person I know best at the table, it looks like he's having hives, like an attack. Everyone else is just slack-jawed. Oh my god, Jane, what did you just do? What did you just do? What did you just do? And they're, like, mouthing this. Or even just saying it out loud, depending on how close they are to me. They all just stared at me and were like, how are you going to fix this for our table?
<v Jane Marie>我转头看去，最熟悉的那个人看起来像是在起荨麻疹，像发作了一样。其他人全都目瞪口呆。天哪，简，你刚才干了什么？你干了什么？你干了什么？他们嘴里嘟囔着，或者直接说了出来，取决于他们离我有多近。他们全都盯着我，仿佛在问：你打算怎么帮我们桌收场？

00:07:56.250 --> 00:08:05.510
<v Elna Baker>Sometimes when something embarrassing happens to you, you exaggerate it in your head, think it's a bigger deal than it was. But in this case, no.
<v Elna Baker>有时当你遇到尴尬的事，你会在脑海中夸大它，认为它比实际情况更严重。但这次，并不是。

00:08:05.510 --> 00:08:18.820
<v Adam Davidson>I wish I could take this away from Jane and tell her her memory's wrong. Her memory's not wrong, because I'm here to tell you, yes, it was that bad. It really was. It was that bad. You're not making that up.
<v Adam Davidson>我真希望能替简分担，告诉她她的记忆出错了。但她的记忆没错，因为我可以告诉你，是的，情况就是那么糟。真的是那么糟。你没有编造。

00:08:18.820 --> 00:08:24.320
<v Elna Baker>That's Adam Davidson, Jane's only friend at the table. He's the guy who broke out into hives.
<v Elna Baker>那位是亚当·戴维森，简同桌唯一的朋友。就是那个起荨麻疹的人。

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:36.770
<v Jane Marie>As I'm remembering it, I just-- that feeling of wah, wah, wah, wah, wah, wah. The universe is different now. The universe is-- fix it, fix it. We're off course. [SIMULATED BUZZING] You know, like that kind of emergency.
<v Jane Marie>如今回忆起来，那种感觉就像是瓦、瓦、瓦……宇宙秩序乱了。宇宙秩序——快修复，快修复。我们偏离轨道了。[模拟嗡嗡声]你知道，就是那种紧急状态。

00:08:36.770 --> 00:08:42.290
<v Elna Baker>Right. So part of the panic is like, if I don't stop this, I'm going to have to pay $25,000.
<v Elna Baker>没错。所以恐慌的一部分在于：如果我不阻止这一切，我就得支付25000美元。

00:08:42.290 --> 00:08:58.840
<v Jane Marie>Uh, yeah, because I said I would. What if there's no way out of this and I have to, like, max out all my credit cards? No, I really was like, can you-- OK, because I don't have auction experience also, I'm like, can you take it back?
<v Jane Marie>嗯，是的，因为我说我会付。如果没法脱身，我岂不是要刷爆所有信用卡？不，我当时真的在想，能不能——好吧，因为我也没参加过拍卖，我在想，能不能撤回？

00:08:58.840 --> 00:09:11.530
<v Elna Baker>She decides to try. At the other end of this giant ballroom, there's an exit. Jane thinks, there will be people in charge there. They can help me. So she bolts up out of her chair and heads there, fast as she can.
<v Elna Baker>她决定试试。在巨大宴会厅的另一头有个出口。简想，那儿肯定有负责人。他们能帮我。于是她迅速起身，向那边跑去。

00:09:11.530 --> 00:09:29.530
<v Jane Marie>Through dozens of tables full of people, I mean, I really was having to be like, excuse me, pardon me, pardon me, excuse me, excuse me, pardon me, pardon me, excuse me. Like just, I gotta get out of here. So I get through the double doors at the back of the room as quickly as I can, and it slams shut behind me.
<v Jane Marie>穿过几十张满是人的桌子，我当时真的不得不一边走一边说：借过，对不起，借过，借过，对不起……我得离开这儿。我迅速穿过后面的双开门，门在我身后“砰”地关上了。

00:09:29.530 --> 00:09:43.800
<v Elna Baker>"Who's running the auction?" she yells. A volunteer points her to a woman with a laptop. Jane rushes over and explains it's all a mistake. I don't have money. The woman is surprisingly understanding. She says, "What would you like to do?"
<v Elna Baker>“谁在负责拍卖？”她大喊。一名志愿者指向一位正在用笔记本电脑的女士。简冲过去解释说一切都是误会。我没钱。那位女士出奇地善解人意。她说：“你想怎么做？”

00:09:43.800 --> 00:10:11.830
<v Jane Marie>What to do you want me to do? And I was like, well, I just need to, like, I take it back. I can't spend that money. And she was like, OK. And then bleep, bleep, blarp, she's in her computer. And I said, thank you, OK. And then I walk back into the ballroom and I'm looking up at the leaderboard, essentially, of all the pledges. And we're at the top. And then it disappears, right as I walk back in the room. It felt like a spotlight was turned on.
<v Jane Marie>你想要我做什么？我说，好吧，我只是需要……我要撤回，我付不起那笔钱。她说，好的。然后噼里啪啦一顿操作，她在电脑上弄好了。我说，谢谢，好的。然后我走回宴会厅，抬头看那块认捐排行榜。我们原本在最上面，就在我走回房间的一瞬间，那个名字消失了。感觉就像聚光灯突然打在了我身上。

00:10:11.830 --> 00:10:13.690
<v Elna Baker>Like the whole room turned around to look at you.
<v Elna Baker>就像整个房间的人都转过头来看你。

00:10:13.690 --> 00:10:21.370
<v Jane Marie>The whole room, it's record scratch. And everyone knows that I just took $25,000 away from this charity.
<v Jane Marie>整个房间，就像唱片刮擦声响起。所有人都知道我刚刚从这个慈善机构撤走了25000美元。

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:27.610
<v Elna Baker>But, of course, it wasn't Jane's name that was on the screen. It was the company's, the one she's hoping to work for.
<v Elna Baker>但当然，屏幕上显示的不是简的名字。而是公司的名字，她原本希望去工作的那家公司。

00:10:27.610 --> 00:10:32.170
<v Adam Davidson>So very quickly it wasn't, oh, Jane did this. It was, our table did this.
<v Adam Davidson>所以很快，大家想的就不是“简做了这事”，而是“我们那桌做了这事”。

00:10:32.170 --> 00:10:34.360
<v Elna Baker>Again, Jane's friend Adam.
<v Elna Baker>又是简的朋友亚当。

00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:51.980
<v Adam Davidson>And it wasn't like we went from first to second or first to third. We went from number 1 to, like, the bottom. So yeah, I was embarrassed. I think everyone at the table was embarrassed. Yes, we were embarrassed. Wouldn't you be embarrassed?
<v Adam Davidson>而且我们不是从第一名降到第二或第三。我们是从第一名直接掉到了榜底。所以是的，我感到很尴尬。我想同桌的每个人都很尴尬。是的，我们都很尴尬。换做是你，你不尴尬吗？

00:10:51.980 --> 00:11:29.000
<v Jane Marie>And so I get back to the table and I sit down and have kind of like a [FORCED LAUGH] "wasn't that"-- you know, like a nervous laugh. No one is enjoying that. And so I just start focusing on anything but the people around my table. I'm just really intently listening to the auctioneer guy. And we have to watch this really sad video that's, like, a mini-documentary about the children that we're supposed to be there to help. And they dim the lights.
<v Jane Marie>所以我回到座位，坐下来，发出了那种[强颜欢笑]“那不是……”你知道，就是一种紧张的笑声。没人觉得有趣。于是我开始专注于除了同桌之外的任何事。我非常专心地听主持人的讲话。接着我们不得不看那段非常悲伤的视频，是一部关于我们本应去帮助的儿童的迷你纪录片。灯光暗了下来。

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:54.820
<v Elna Baker>The movie is about 15 minutes long. The music is emotional. It's parents and their children with epilepsy talking about how scared and sad they feel, how much they need treatment. Sitting there in the dark watching this, this is the moment that hits Jane the hardest. It's not when everyone looks at her. It's when she realizes what she's actually done. She'd only thought of this evening as an opportunity for her to make an impression.
<v Elna Baker>电影大约有15分钟长。音乐很煽情。画面中是患有癫痫的孩子和他们的父母，诉说着他们的恐惧和悲伤，以及他们是多么需要治疗。坐在黑暗中看着这一切，这是对简冲击最大的一刻。不是当每个人盯着她看的时候，而是当她意识到自己真正做了什么的时候。她一直把今晚视为自己表现的机会。

00:11:54.820 --> 00:12:22.660
<v Jane Marie>That was gross, what I did. It was gross and wrong. And watching these children with this illness, on a big screen, I was just like, I am the worst person on the Earth, that I bid and then I took it back, and then I said no. [SNIFFLE] There's was a part of me that was like, oh, could I just take out a few more credit cards or something?
<v Jane Marie>我做的事真是太恶劣了。太恶劣而且错了。在大屏幕上看到这些患病的孩子，我当时想，我是世界上最糟糕的人，我竞拍了然后又撤回，我拒绝了。[抽泣] 有一部分我在想，我是不是可以多刷几张信用卡之类的？

00:12:22.660 --> 00:12:41.160
<v Jane Marie>And then it's like a tidal wave of what just happened and what I did and how mortifying it is. Just the layers of, like, the reasons I should be embarrassed and criticizing myself. You know, why would you even do that? Why would you do that? And then when you did do it, why would you try to get out of it?
<v Jane Marie>然后就像一阵海啸袭来，想到刚刚发生的事，想到我做了什么，那种感觉太羞耻了。层层叠叠的、我应该感到尴尬和自我批评的理由。你知道，你为什么要那样做？你为什么要那样做？而且当你做了之后，为什么要试图逃避？

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:51.720
<v Elna Baker>The answers to those questions are uncomfortable because they force Jane to see things about herself she doesn't like. Embarrassment can do that. It can show you your blind spot.
<v Elna Baker>这些问题的答案让人不安，因为它们迫使简直视自己身上不喜欢的特质。尴尬能做到这一点。它能让你看到你的盲点。

00:12:51.720 --> 00:13:01.620
<v Jane Marie>It exposed a fatal flaw that I have to pay a lot of attention to now.
<v Jane Marie>它暴露了我现在必须非常关注的一个致命弱点。

00:13:01.620 --> 00:13:04.000
<v Elna Baker>And what is that fatal flaw?
<v Elna Baker>那个致命弱点是什么？

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:17.470
<v Jane Marie>Um, hubris. Something about that made me feel like my confidence had bad consequences, that there was something bad about it.
<v Jane Marie>嗯，狂妄自大。那件事中的某些东西让我觉得，我的自信带来了糟糕的后果，这其中有些不好的成分。

00:13:17.470 --> 00:13:29.980
<v Elna Baker>The night ends. She doesn't get the job. And she has it on pretty good authority that the decision boiled down to her big mistake that night. This stings, but that's not the crux of what that night did to her.
<v Elna Baker>夜晚结束了。她没得到那份工作。她有相当可靠的消息来源确认，决定取决于她当晚犯下的那个大错。这很痛，但这并不是那个晚上带给她的核心影响。

00:13:29.980 --> 00:13:40.640
<v Jane Marie>It has actually changed a lot about how I act in public. I'm just much more self-conscious after that.
<v Jane Marie>它确实改变了我很多在公共场合的行为方式。那之后我变得非常拘谨。

00:13:40.640 --> 00:13:41.380
<v Elna Baker>Really?
<v Elna Baker>真的吗？

00:13:41.380 --> 00:14:12.710
<v Jane Marie>Like, I used to get super pumped about going to a party. And now that's kind of fraught. I know this is going to sound counterintuitive, but I've started leaving the house sometimes without makeup because it's like I don't want people thinking that I'm thinking about what they're thinking, if that makes sense. I don't want anyone to think I put makeup on for them. But this is just like the weirdest stuff that now pops into my head about what other people are thinking, where I wish I just didn't care.
<v Jane Marie>比如，我以前去派对会特别兴奋。现在那变得有点让人忐忑。我知道这听起来很反直觉，但我现在开始有时不化妆就出门，因为我不想让别人觉得我在意他们的看法，如果这说得通的话。我不想让任何人觉得我是为他们而化妆。但这些奇怪的想法现在总是跳进我的脑海，关于别人在想什么，我真希望自己能不在乎。

00:14:12.710 --> 00:14:23.480
<v Elna Baker>I've always been that way, worrying what other people are thinking about me. And I can't imagine what it would be like to discover this as a full-on adult. Jane says everything feels different to her now.
<v Elna Baker>我一直都是这样，担心别人怎么看我。我无法想象作为一个完全成熟的成年人发现这一点会是什么感觉。简说现在一切对她来说都不同了。

00:14:23.480 --> 00:14:26.870
<v Jane Marie>I feel completely neutered a lot of the time.
<v Jane Marie>我常常觉得完全被阉割了一样。

00:14:26.870 --> 00:14:27.620
<v Elna Baker>Really?
<v Elna Baker>真的？

00:14:27.620 --> 00:14:57.350
<v Jane Marie>Yeah, I do. I feel like it made me second guess myself a lot, I guess. I used to write essays about parenting and sex. And I would give advice and I would go on radio shows. And now I'm just like, shut up, Jane, shut up, Jane, shut up, Jane, shut up, Jane, shut up. No one is asking and no one wants to hear it.
<v Jane Marie>是的，我确实这么觉得。我觉得那让我对自己产生了很多质疑。我以前写关于育儿和性的文章，我给出建议，我上电台节目。而现在我只是想：闭嘴吧简，闭嘴吧简，闭嘴吧简，没有人问你，也没人想听。

00:14:57.350 --> 00:15:11.390
<v Elna Baker>That's crazy to me that you can do something embarrassing once, and suddenly you have that inner policeman for the rest of your life. Is there anything that can make it go away, you think?
<v Elna Baker>对我来说，因为一次尴尬就突然拥有了余生的“内心警察”，这太疯狂了。你认为有什么能让它消失吗？

00:15:11.390 --> 00:15:30.240
<v Jane Marie>I don't know. What could change it? I don't know. I just don't even have the desire. It's like, why would I want to change it, so that I could, like, put another ball gown on and go embarrass myself in front of a bunch of people? Like no, that doesn't sound fun. You know?
<v Jane Marie>不知道。什么能改变它？不知道。我甚至都没那种渴望。就像，我为什么要改变它，好让我能再穿上礼服，在一堆人面前让自己尴尬一次？不，那听起来一点也不好玩，你知道吗？

00:15:30.240 --> 00:15:55.010
<v Jane Marie>It doesn't sound fun anymore. Like, I don't think that the lesson I learned was necessarily all bad. And honestly, when I think about the old version of me, it was kind of gross, in a way, you know? It's kind of obnoxious. It's like, that's a baby.
<v Jane Marie>那听起来不再有趣了。而且老实说，我不认为我学到的教训全是坏事。坦白讲，当我想到过去的那个我，在某种程度上确实挺恶心的，你知道吗？挺令人讨厌的。就像个婴儿一样。

00:15:55.010 --> 00:16:36.510
<v Elna Baker>I'd honestly never thought of embarrassment as being helpful to anyone. It pummels us, humiliates us. But Jane's glad this happened to her. She's glad for her new, awful self-consciousness. She says her ego is smaller now, which means that with everyone in her life, she's quicker to admit when she's wrong and apologize. And overall, she says it's calmed her down. She observes more, hangs back, never wants to be the center of attention-- which, she says, is a lot less hectic.
<v Elna Baker>老实说，我从未想过尴尬对任何人有帮助。它击垮我们，羞辱我们。但简很高兴这件事发生在自己身上。她很高兴自己有了新的、可怕的自觉性。她说她现在的自我意识变小了，这意味着生活中无论对谁，她都能更快地承认错误并道歉。总体而言，她说这让她冷静下来了。她观察得更多，不再冲在前面，再也不想成为关注的焦点——这让她觉得轻松多了。

00:16:36.510 --> 00:16:46.320
<v Elna Baker>Jane Marie is the host of the podcast The Dream.
<v Elna Baker>简·玛丽是播客《梦想》的主持人。

00:16:46.320 --> 00:17:20.020
<v Elna Baker>Act 2, Judgment Day. Part of the reason I'm interested in embarrassment is because of this realization I had recently. I'm so embarrassed of myself all the time. At least once a day, I break out into a rash from embarrassment, like a couple of days ago when I learned from a coworker that all my life I've been mispronouncing the word "incongruous." See? I get embarrassed when I think people are looking at my eyebrows or when other people see my dog poop. Embarrassment is on me all the time, and I want to get it off.
<v Elna Baker>第二幕，审判日。我对比尴尬感兴趣的部分原因，是因为我最近的一个发现。我总是对自己感到非常尴尬。至少每天一次，我会因为尴尬而起疹子，就像几天前，我从同事那里得知我这一生都把“incongruous”（不协调的）这个词念错了。你看？当我以为别人在看我的眉毛时，或者当别人看到我遛狗留下的粪便时，我都会感到尴尬。尴尬时刻伴随着我，我想要摆脱它。

00:17:20.020 --> 00:17:37.330
<v Elna Baker>And one of the ways my obsession with embarrassment has played out in the last few months is I'm in a group chat dedicated to it. There are four of us in it. One of the people, Kiese Laymon, I only met when we started this group. Any time we feel embarrassed or do something embarrassing, we write to each other.
<v Elna Baker>过去几个月里，我对尴尬的痴迷以一种方式呈现出来：我加入了一个专门讨论尴尬的群聊。我们有四个人。其中一个人，基斯·莱蒙，是我加入这个群后才认识的。每当我们感到尴尬或做了尴尬的事，我们就会写给彼此。

00:17:37.330 --> 00:18:04.460
<v Elna Baker>It's how I know we've both worn Spanx for decades, or that he's self-conscious of his hairline or how he walks. These little secrets, you'd never know about someone otherwise. Sometimes we swap stories of past embarrassments, like the time Kiese was teaching a class at Vassar College on hip-hop as literature, and the alumni magazine wanted to do a story on him. This was back when he was in his 20s. They did the interview, and then a photographer comes to take his picture.
<v Elna Baker>所以我才知道我们俩都已经穿了几十年的塑身衣了，或者他对于自己的发际线或走路姿势很在意。这些小秘密，否则你永远不会了解某人。有时我们会交换过去的尴尬故事，比如基斯在瓦萨学院教嘻哈文学课时，校友杂志想给他写篇报道。那还是在他20多岁的时候。采访做完了，摄影师来给他拍照。

00:18:04.460 --> 00:18:23.240
<v Kiese Laymon>So, we're in the library, and I'm like, where do you need me to stand? And they're like, well, could you crouch? And I was like, huh? I ain't never crouched for the photographs before. And they were like, you know, crouch and, like, do something cool with your hands. And I was like, what do you-- what do you want me to do?
<v Kiese Laymon>我们在图书馆，我问：需要我站在哪里？他们说：嗯，你能蹲下来吗？我当时心想：啊？我以前拍照从来没蹲过。他们说：你知道，蹲下，然后用手摆出个酷酷的姿势。我问：你……你想要我做什么？

00:18:23.240 --> 00:18:56.650
<v Kiese Laymon>And then the lady says, could you do rap hands? And I'm just like, wait a minute now. You want me to do rap hands in a picture that's going to accompany an article about my teaching hip-hop as literature and hip-hop and literature? And you know my stupid ass did that shit? Like, I'm doing rap hands, fam. Like, I can't lie to you.
<v Kiese Laymon>然后那位女士说：你能摆出“饶舌手势”吗？我当时想：等等。你想要我在一篇关于我教授嘻哈作为文学，以及嘻哈与文学的文章配图里，做“饶舌手势”？结果我这笨蛋真的做了那事？我真的摆出了饶舌手势，兄弟。我没法对你撒谎。

00:18:56.650 --> 00:19:20.800
<v Kiese Laymon>So then the fucking article comes out and my students, particularly my Black students, are like, bruh, you teach about representation, stereotypes, fetishization. There's nothing ironic about this picture. You look like a fucking fool, wannabe rapper/professor doing crouch rap hands.
<v Kiese Laymon>结果该死的文章刊登了，我的学生，特别是黑人学生说：兄弟，你教的是代表性、刻板印象、恋物化。这张照片一点也不讽刺。你看起来像个彻头彻尾的傻瓜，一个想当饶舌歌手的教授在蹲着做饶舌手势。

00:19:20.800 --> 00:19:28.840
<v Elna Baker>I know so many of Kiese's day-to-day screw-ups from our embarrassment group. But it's funny. I never asked him an obvious question.
<v Elna Baker>通过尴尬群，我知道了基斯很多日常的蠢事。但很有趣，我从未问过他一个显而易见的问题。

00:19:28.840 --> 00:19:43.320
<v Elna Baker>All right, so I guess-- I think-- you know, I'm coming into this totally blind, so I don't know what you're about to tell me. What's your most embarrassing story?
<v Elna Baker>好的，那么我猜……我想……你知道，我对此一无所知，我不知道你要告诉我什么。你最尴尬的故事是什么？

00:19:43.320 --> 00:20:11.200
<v Kiese Laymon>My most embarrassing story has to do with Heavy. So I wrote this book called Heavy. I've been spending my entire life trying to write it. And I needed to write it to help my relationship with my mother and my relationship with my body. But also, it's a book that I wanted my grandmama to really be proud of.
<v Kiese Laymon>我最尴尬的故事与《沉重》（Heavy）有关。我写了这本书叫《沉重》。我花了一辈子时间试图写它。我需要写它来改善我与母亲的关系，以及我与自己身体的关系。但同时，这也是一本我想让祖母真正为我骄傲的书。《纽约时报》将《沉重》列为过去50年最好的50本回忆录之一。书中大部分内容是关于基斯对他体重的羞耻感。他从319磅变得厌食，减到了159磅。然后又胖了回去，再减掉一些。通过暴露他与体重抗争的一切，他克服了它。在书的结尾，他写道：“我不再为这个沉重的黑人身体感到羞耻。”

00:20:11.200 --> 00:20:39.000
<v Elna Baker>The New York Times names Heavy one of the best 50 memoirs in the last 50 years. A lot of the book is about Kiese's shame around his weight. He went from being 319 pounds to anorexic, getting down to 159. Then gaining it back, then losing some. By exposing everything about his struggle with his weight, he overcomes it. Near the end of the book, he writes, "I am no longer ashamed of this heavy Black body."
<v Elna Baker>（接上）他还谈到祖母对他有多重要。她很幽默，是他生命中的理性之声。她是唯一从不拿他的体重说事的人之一。在《沉重》中，他这样写祖母：“她负责任地爱我，从没伤害过我。”

00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:55.860
<v Elna Baker>Something else he talks about, how important his grandma is to him. She's funny, the voice of reason in his life. And she's one of the only people who's never on his case about his weight. In Heavy, he writes about his grandmother, "She responsibly loved me and never harmed me."
<v Elna Baker>他让她失望过很多次——被大学开除，赌博输光了所有钱。但写这本书是他证明自己并非一无是处的方式，也是他试图完全解释自己。

00:20:55.860 --> 00:21:08.810
<v Elna Baker>He's disappointed her many times-- got kicked out of college, lost all his money to gambling. But writing this book was a way to prove to her he's not his failures. And it's his attempt to fully explain himself.
<v Elna Baker>到书出版时，我想我已经赢得了许多我不曾预期会赢得的奖项。每次获奖，我都会想：哟，我奶奶一定会为我骄傲的。

00:21:08.810 --> 00:21:22.330
<v Kiese Laymon>By the time it comes out, I think I had won a lot of awards that I did not expect to win. And with every award, I just was like, yo, my granny is going to be proud of me.
<v Kiese Laymon>书出版两个月后，他飞回密西西比州杰克逊市过圣诞节。这是他第一次有机会与全家人一起庆祝书的成功。他带了一摞书，每一本都签了献给家人的寄语。他带着大大的笑容跑进客厅。所有人都在那儿。他径直走向祖母，给了她一个大大的拥抱。

00:21:22.330 --> 00:21:43.440
<v Elna Baker>Two months after the book is published, he flies home to Jackson, Mississippi, for Christmas. It's the first chance he gets to celebrate the book's success with his whole family. And he brings a stack of books, each of which he's signed with a dedication to each family member. He runs into the living room with a big smile on his face. Everyone's there. He heads straight to his grandma and gives her a big hug.
<v Elna Baker>她抬头看着我，然后看向我妈，说：这是基吗？然后她说：是啊，妈，这是基。我说：奶奶，你问我是不是我是什么意思？她直截了当地说：因为你太胖了，宝贝。你看起来胖得不像基了。她当着全家人的面说了这话。她当着我小表妹妮可的面说了。

00:21:43.440 --> 00:22:15.570
<v Kiese Laymon>She looks up at me, and then she looks over to my mom and she says, is this Kie? And then she's like, yeah, Mama, that's Kie. And I said, Grandmama, what you asking am I me for? And she just straight-up said, because you're so fat, baby. You look way too fat to be Kie. And she said it in front of my family. She said in front of my little cousin, Nicole.
<v Kiese Laymon>我开始冒冷汗。我当时心想：搞什么鬼，搞什么鬼？我正在恐慌发作吗？当时，我不明白为什么。我没觉得是因为祖母叫我胖子而感到尴尬或羞耻导致的恐慌。

00:22:15.570 --> 00:22:33.060
<v Kiese Laymon>And I just started sweating. And I was like, what the fuck, what the fuck? Am I having a panic attack right now? And at the time, I didn't understand why. Like, I didn't think I was having a panic attack because of embarrassment or shame at my grandmother calling me fat.
<v Kiese Laymon>这是一种羞辱、羞耻和尴尬的结合。羞耻和尴尬经常被混为一谈。但尴尬的独特性在于它总是与他人相关。你需要观众才能感到尴尬。

00:22:33.060 --> 00:22:47.260
<v Elna Baker>It was some combination of humiliation, shame, and embarrassment. Shame and embarrassment often get lumped together. But what makes embarrassment distinct is it's always tied to other people. You need an audience to be embarrassed.
<v Elna Baker>就像，我可以独自在家里感到羞耻。

00:22:47.260 --> 00:22:51.570
<v Kiese Laymon>Like, I can be ashamed in my house by myself.
<v Kiese Laymon>是的，绝对。

00:22:51.570 --> 00:22:52.930
<v Elna Baker>Yes, totally.
<v Elna Baker>你知道我在说什么吗？我可以处在羞耻中，就像在羞耻的锅里煮。但尴尬对我来说更具戏剧性。我觉得，当你问我尴尬故事时，那全都是关于某人看到了我的故事。

00:22:52.930 --> 00:23:17.290
<v Kiese Laymon>You know what I'm saying? Like, I can be in shame, like, a boiling pot of shame. But embarrassment, to me, is much more dramatic. I feel like, when you ask me stories about embarrassment, they're all going to be stories about how somebody saw me.
<v Kiese Laymon>因为如果我回到家，屋里除了奶奶没别人，她说了那话，会很受伤。我的意思是，那会伤人。但绝不会像她在全家人面前说出来那样伤害我。而且家人们都在表现出他们那种“向下看”的姿态。你知道，大家都听到了，但我们要假装没听到。

00:23:17.290 --> 00:23:52.540
<v Kiese Laymon>Because if I came in my house and there was nobody there but my Granny and she did that, it would hurt. I mean, it would hurt. But it would not come close to that hurt of having her say it in front of the entire family. And the family doing their version of looking down. You know, we're going to act like we didn't hear that, when everybody heard it.
<v Kiese Laymon>所以我直接走出了屋子，钻进车里，努力调整呼吸。因为之前做过书的巡回宣传，我手里有阿普唑仑（Xanax），我用它在飞机上睡觉。[沉重的叹息] 我当时想：哟，我要不要吃半片阿普唑仑？到底是怎么回事？我不知道发生了什么。所以我没吃。我吃了阿普唑仑，然后又回到屋里，试着再来一次。

00:23:52.540 --> 00:24:22.220
<v Kiese Laymon>So I just walked right back out of the house, got in my car for a second, tried to get my breath right. And I had some Xanax because I was on book tour earlier, and I use Xanax to sleep on the planes. [HEAVY SIGH] So I was like, yo, should I take a half a Xanax? What the fuck is going on with me? I didn't know what was happening. So I didn't. I took the Xanax, one of the Xanax, and I went back in the house, tried to do it again.
<v Kiese Laymon>回到奶奶身边。走进房间：奶奶，嘿，那个那个……基，孩子，我不知道你怎么变得这么胖，但你需要停止进食了。我觉得羞耻的部分在于：无论我写多少书，无论我把这些书写得有多好，但在我的家人，以及那个世界上对我最重要的人眼中，我看起来和感觉上不仅是胖，而且是恶心。

00:24:22.220 --> 00:24:55.570
<v Kiese Laymon>Went back to my Granny. Walked in the room, Granny, hey, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah. Kie, boy, I don't know how you got so fat, but you need to stop eating. And I think the shameful part was, like, I could write all the books in the world I wanted to. I could write one of those books as good as I could ever write them. But I was still going to look and feel not just fat, but gross, to my family.
<v Kiese Laymon>你是否也觉得，在写书并竭尽全力去审视这些东西的过程中，你已经克服了那种对自己感到极度羞耻的能力？

00:24:55.570 --> 00:25:09.740
<v Elna Baker>Did you also feel like you had, in writing a book and working as hard as you possibly could to look at this stuff, that you had overcome the capacity to feel so ashamed of yourself?
<v Elna Baker>是的。我以为，通过撰写身体、性、胖、性等内容，我已经做到了。我骗自己相信我已经从中解脱了。我想当我走进那个房间，以为《沉重》会保护我不受他们的批评，却遭到了那样的欢迎，还有祖母叫我胖子，看着我像在看一个胖子。当她看我时，除了一个中年大胖子她什么也看不到，那是氪石，是我的氪石。

00:25:09.740 --> 00:25:59.840
<v Kiese Laymon>Yes. I thought, in writing about, among other things, body and sex, fatness and sex, I did. I fooled myself into believing that I was delivered from it. And I think when I came in the room thinking Heavy would shield me from their critiques, and being greeted, initially, and then my grandmother calling me fat, looking at me like I'm fat. Not seeing anything but a fat ass middle-aged man when she looks at me is kryptonite, is my kryptonite.
<v Kiese Laymon>她是唯一从不评判他的人。当他试图理解这种转变时，他能想到的唯一解释是：随着书的成功和带来的财富，她现在把他看作一个男人了。在她眼中，男人应该多产且精干。他在奶奶家待了几天，她不断提起这件事。好像基吃了基。他胖得像房子一样。他怎么胖成这样了？

00:25:59.840 --> 00:26:25.960
<v Elna Baker>She'd always been the one person who'd never judged him. And when he tries to make sense of this turnaround, the best he can come up with is, with the success of the book and the money it brought in, she now saw him as a man. And in her eyes, men were supposed to be productive and lean. He spends the next few days with his grandma, and she keeps bringing it up. It looks like Kie ate Kie. He's as big as a house. How did he get so fat?
<v Elna Baker>我觉得这很尴尬，因为我天真地以为一本书能改变我的人生，让我不再在家人面前、不再在那个对我最重要的人面前看起来很胖。而那本书其实一点作用都没有。

00:26:25.960 --> 00:26:39.370
<v Kiese Laymon>And I just think that was embarrassing, to think that a book was going to change your life and make you not look and feel fat to your family and to the person who means the most in the world to you. And the book didn't matter at all.
<v Kiese Laymon>噢，这真是摧毁性的。

00:26:39.370 --> 00:26:40.540
<v Elna Baker>Oh, it's crushing.
<v Elna Baker>[笑] 噢，这真好笑。我不得不笑着讲完这一切。但是的，这感觉相当摧毁。

00:26:40.540 --> 00:26:51.610
<v Kiese Laymon>[LAUGHS] Oh, it's so funny. I have to laugh my way through all of that. But yeah, it felt pretty crushing.
<v Kiese Laymon>但他平时并不会告诉人们这些事。老实说，当我请他讲最尴尬的故事时，我期待的是那种你在派对上讲的幽默故事。通常当人们开始讲这类故事时，他会讲出类似“饶舌手势”那样的内容。

00:26:51.610 --> 00:27:07.150
<v Elna Baker>But this isn't something he tells people in everyday conversation. Honestly, when I asked him for his most embarrassing story, I expected the kind of funny story you tell at a party. That's usually what he delivers when people start telling these kinds of stories, something more like Rap Hands.
<v Elna Baker>那种尴尬和我奶奶家感受到的尴尬，在某种程度上，我甚至不知道它们是否该用同一个词来形容。因为我真的……我是说，我喜欢讲那个饶舌的故事。我从没讲过我告诉你关于奶奶的那个故事，因为我不喜欢回想那个。我讨厌讲它。你懂我的意思吗？

00:27:07.150 --> 00:27:28.760
<v Kiese Laymon>That embarrassment and the embarrassment I felt in my granny's house, in some way, I don't even know if they should be called the same word. Because I literally-- like, I like telling that story. I've never told the story I told you about my granny because I don't like remembering that. I don't like telling it. You know what I'm saying?
<v Kiese Laymon>完全懂。我是说，有些事你可以一笑置之。但你无法笑对那些让你想死的事情。

00:27:28.760 --> 00:27:37.950
<v Elna Baker>Totally. I mean, because there's certain things that you can laugh at. But you can't laugh at the thing that makes you want to die.
<v Elna Baker>是的，我是说我讲的尴尬故事往往不是真实的。故事的戏剧性可能是真的，但我会隐藏那个糊涂、有时带有毒性的核心，即认为讲尴尬故事会让人们更喜欢我。你知道我的意思吗？

00:27:37.950 --> 00:28:04.280
<v Kiese Laymon>Yeah, I mean the embarrassing stories that I tell are always not true. Like, the drama of the story will be true, but I'm going to hide that mushy, sometimes toxic center that thinks telling embarrassing stories will make people like me more. You know what I mean?
<v Kiese Laymon>所以当我讲尴尬故事时，我没有讲的部分是：我要讲给你们听是因为我内心极度不自信，我想要让你们感觉什么或者发笑。所以如果我不讲那部分，我在讲故事时就是不诚实的。

00:28:04.280 --> 00:28:19.720
<v Kiese Laymon>So when I'm telling embarrassing stories, the part of the story I'm not telling is, I'm going to tell you all this story because I feel very fucking insecure and I want to make you feel or laugh. So if I'm not telling that part of the story, I'm being dishonest in the storytelling.
<v Kiese Laymon>而且我们大多数人在讲这些自嘲故事时，都没有讲出那一面。我讲这个故事只是为了控制你们喜欢我的进度。我认为那些故事缺乏的就是这个。它们缺乏前奏。

00:28:19.720 --> 00:28:42.030
<v Kiese Laymon>And most of us aren't telling that part of the story when we tell these self-deprecating stories about whatever. I'm only telling you this story because I want to control the velocity and pace at which you like me. That's what I think those stories lack. They lack the preamble.
<v Kiese Laymon>它们缺乏让你在第一时间需要讲一个搞笑故事的悲伤历史，他说。也许这有道理。谁会想在派对上提起那个？

00:28:42.030 --> 00:28:59.540
<v Elna Baker>They lack the sad history that makes you need to tell a funny story in the first place, he says. And maybe that makes sense. Who wants to bring that up at a party?
<v Elna Baker>基斯·莱蒙是《沉重》和《长除法》的作者。接下来，一场以裸体告终的约会，但绝不是你可能想要的那种。一会儿回来，芝加哥公共广播电台，我们的节目继续。

00:28:59.540 --> 00:29:21.210
<v Elna Baker>Kiese Laymon is the author of Heavy and Long Division. Coming up, a date that ends in nudity, but absolutely not the kind that you might want. That's in a minute, from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Elna Baker>这是《美国生活》。我是艾尔娜·贝克，代班伊拉·格拉斯。今天的节目主题是“我的错”。今天我们来看看尴尬的故事以及它们揭示了什么。我们来到了第三幕，“创伤乐园”。就像我之前提到的，我们邀请听众分享你们最尴尬的故事。我们收到了数百条Facebook和Instagram留言，还有近一千封邮件。

00:29:21.210 --> 00:29:42.310
<v Elna Baker>It's This American Life. I'm Elna Baker, sitting in for Ira Glass. Today's show, "My Bad." Today we're looking at embarrassing stories and what those reveal about us. We're at Act 3, Traumarama. Like I mentioned earlier, we asked you listeners to share your most embarrassing stories. We got hundreds of Facebook and Instagram messages and nearly a thousand emails.
<v Elna Baker>好的，提醒一下，尴尬的故事有时涉及生理机能，我们有一两个类似的内容。如果这不是你喜欢的，别说我没提醒你。总之，这些寄来的故事，我请了本节目的常驻主持人伊拉·格拉斯来一起分享。

00:29:42.310 --> 00:29:57.090
<v Elna Baker>OK, quick note, embarrassing stories can sometimes involve bodily functions, and we have one or two like that. If that's not your thing, don't say I didn't warn you. Anyway, these stories you sent in, I invited the regular host of this show, Ira Glass, in to share those.
<v Elna Baker>嘿，艾尔娜。

00:29:57.090 --> 00:29:57.730
<v Ira Glass>Hey, there, Elna.
<v Ira Glass>嗨，伊拉。

00:29:57.730 --> 00:29:58.840
<v Elna Baker>Hi, Ira.
<v Elna Baker>我能问你个问题吗？

00:29:58.840 --> 00:30:01.480
<v Ira Glass>So can I ask you something?
<v Ira Glass>可以。

00:30:01.480 --> 00:30:01.840
<v Elna Baker>Yeah.
<v Elna Baker>OK，你之前说你每天都会感到尴尬。真的吗？

00:30:01.840 --> 00:30:07.570
<v Ira Glass>OK, you said earlier that you get embarrassed every day. Really?
<v Ira Glass>噢，当然。根据这个节目的录制进度，两天前我发生了一件非常尴尬的事。

00:30:07.570 --> 00:30:12.740
<v Elna Baker>Oh, yeah. I had something really embarrassing happen to me two nights ago, per this show.
<v Elna Baker>是什么？

00:30:12.740 --> 00:30:13.970
<v Ira Glass>What was it?
<v Ira Glass>从摔倒开始。我端着一盘披萨，结果绊倒了，披萨面朝下掉在沙发上。

00:30:13.970 --> 00:30:22.430
<v Elna Baker>It started with tripping and falling. I was carrying a pizza on a plate and I tripped, and it fell face-forward onto the couch.
<v Elna Baker>嗯哼。这是在你家？

00:30:22.430 --> 00:30:24.630
<v Ira Glass>Uh-huh. This is in your own home?
<v Ira Glass>不，是在我约会的那个男人的家里。那是张白沙发，所以会留下污渍。我正在清理它。然后发现了一块完美的马苏里拉芝士卷。你知道，它掉下来了，我不该吃的。但我还是想吃。所以我把它从沙发缝里捡出来，吃了。就在我咬下去的时候，我意识到——

00:30:24.630 --> 00:30:46.730
<v Elna Baker>No, this is at the guy I'm seeing, right. And it was, like, a white couch, so it stains. So I'm cleaning it up. And then there was a perfect piece of mozzarella curl. And, you know, it fell, so I shouldn't have eaten it. But I wanted to eat it. So I picked it up out of the-- it was stuck in the couch. I picked it up and ate it. And as I bit in, I realized--
<v Elna Baker>[笑] 那不是——

00:30:46.730 --> 00:30:49.280
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHS] It was not--
<v Ira Glass>那是一个完整的脚趾甲，很大一个脚趾甲。

00:30:49.280 --> 00:30:54.000
<v Elna Baker>It was a full toenail, like, a big toe toenail.
<v Elna Baker>（笑）什么？

00:30:54.000 --> 00:30:55.480
<v Ira Glass>(LAUGHING) What?
<v Ira Glass>真是……

00:30:55.480 --> 00:30:56.630
<v Elna Baker>It was so--
<v Elna Baker>那怎么会看起来像马苏里拉？马苏里拉是有立体感的。脚趾甲？

00:30:56.630 --> 00:31:02.770
<v Ira Glass>How does that look like mozzarella? Mozzarella has three dimensions to it. A toenail?
<v Ira Glass>（笑）那是我做过最恶心的事。

00:31:02.770 --> 00:31:07.740
<v Elna Baker>(LAUGHING) It was the grossest thing I've ever done.
<v Elna Baker>我觉得这不该怪你。那是他的错，他居然把脚趾甲留在沙发里。他才该感到尴尬。

00:31:07.740 --> 00:31:14.720
<v Ira Glass>I don't think that's on you. I think that that's on him for leaving the toenail in the couch. He should have been embarrassed.
<v Ira Glass>是啊，但我把脚趾甲捡起来放进嘴里，这一系列事件发生的时机简直天衣无缝。

00:31:14.720 --> 00:31:28.610
<v Elna Baker>Yeah, but the needle that needed to be threaded for me to pick up a toenail and put it into my mouth, the sequence of events that would be the only reason I did that, happened seamlessly.
<v Elna Baker>他在看吗？

00:31:28.610 --> 00:31:29.690
<v Ira Glass>And was he watching?
<v Ira Glass>他在看。他也感到尴尬。他非常尴尬。

00:31:29.690 --> 00:31:33.430
<v Elna Baker>Yeah. He was embarrassed too. He was very embarrassed.
<v Elna Baker>因为那是他的脚趾甲。

00:31:33.430 --> 00:31:34.770
<v Ira Glass>Because it was his toenail.
<v Ira Glass>是的。他说：“这看起来不像我的。” 我说：“那除了你还能是谁的？”

00:31:34.770 --> 00:31:42.660
<v Elna Baker>Yeah. He goes, that just doesn't-- it just doesn't seem like me. I was like, well, whose toenail is it then?
<v Elna Baker>是的，有闯入者进了你家，剪了指甲，什么都没偷，然后就走了。我在《纽约邮报》读到过，这种事经常发生。

00:31:42.660 --> 00:31:52.370
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, somebody broke into your apartment, cut their nails, didn't take anything, and left. I've been reading about that in the New York Post, happening all the time.
<v Ira Glass>OK，但我们不是来讨论我的。我有这么多听众寄来的尴尬故事想和你分享。

00:31:52.370 --> 00:31:59.240
<v Elna Baker>OK, but we're not here to talk about me. We have all of these listener embarrassing stories I want to share with you.
<v Elna Baker>是的，我很好奇你发现了什么。

00:31:59.240 --> 00:32:01.580
<v Ira Glass>Yes, and I am very curious about what you found.
<v Ira Glass>好的，从这些邮件中我最突出的发现之一是，很多人写信说：“我肯定是世界上唯一一个会干这种事的人。” 但五封邮件过后，另一个人就在收件箱里发来了同样的故事。

00:32:01.580 --> 00:32:15.940
<v Elna Baker>All right, so one of the things that stood out to me most from reading all these emails is that so many people wrote in saying, I'm the only person who could have possibly done this. And then five emails later was someone in the inbox with the exact same story.
