00:34:08.690 --> 00:34:15.050
<v Elna Baker>Yeah. And this is, like, during the height of the AIDS epidemic. Our country actually banned people from Haiti from entering the US.
<v Elna Baker>因为艾滋病。

00:34:15.050 --> 00:34:16.100
<v Ira Glass>Because of AIDS.
<v Ira Glass>没错。

00:34:16.100 --> 00:34:16.390
<v Elna Baker>Exactly.
<v Elna Baker>对。

00:34:16.390 --> 00:34:17.060
<v Ira Glass>Right.
<v Ira Glass>深夜电视节目里到处是关于海地人的笑话。所以在中学，威尔一直因为是海地人而被找麻烦。他被打，被嘲笑。

00:34:17.060 --> 00:34:26.960
<v Elna Baker>And there were jokes about Haitians on late night TV. And so, in middle school, Will is picked on all the time for being Haitian. He's beat up. He's teased.
<v Elna Baker>所以我上高中了，九年级，新的一年，新的地点。离家很远。新的孩子，新的老师，九年级一切顺利。过了两个月，交了一些朋友。然后，有个孩子问我：“哟，你有口音，哪儿来的？” 我心想：噢，该死，又来了。

00:34:26.960 --> 00:34:49.480
<v Will>So boom, I go to high school, ninth grade, new year, new location. This is a location far away from my home. New kids, new teachers, ninth grade going well. Two months in, getting some friends. And then, one of the kids asked me, yo, you got an accent. Where you from? I'm like, oh, shit, here we go again.
<v Will>所有的孩子都在等回应。我脱口而出：“哟，我是牙买加人。” 他们说：“噢，哇，牙买加人。” 我当时想：噢，该死，现在我余生都得当牙买加人了。我记得在和人交谈时，我会故意加入牙买加俚语，比如“哟，你做作业了吗？”之类的。只是为了维持这种牙买加形象。

00:34:49.480 --> 00:35:23.310
<v Will>And so there's all the kids waiting for a response. And I just remember, I'm like, yo, I'm Jamaican. And they was like, oh, whoa, yeah, mon, eh, bope, bope, bope. And I was like, oh shit, now I got to be Jamaican for the rest of my life. And I just remember, during my conversations with people, I would just throw in Jamaican phrases like, yo, did you do you homework, bope bope? Where you going? Yo, I didn't understand the lesson. Blood clot. You know, just saying that, just to keep up this Jamaican image.
<v Will>听起来你甚至不太了解当牙买加人是怎么回事。

00:35:23.310 --> 00:35:26.950
<v Elna Baker>It sounds to me like you didn't even really know much about being Jamaican.
<v Elna Baker>我什么都不了解……我了解的极少。我只知道他们的口音和音乐，我只是把刻板印象拿来用了，然后变成了那样。

00:35:26.950 --> 00:35:42.520
<v Will>I didn't know anything about being-- I knew very little about being Jamaican. I knew how they sounded from their music, but it was all just-- I took the stereotypes of Jamaicans and I became that.
<v Will>所以他装了三年。

00:35:42.520 --> 00:35:45.420
<v Elna Baker>So he pulls this lie off for three years.
<v Elna Baker>哇。

00:35:45.420 --> 00:35:46.090
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>是的。在学校里，他就是那个牙买加小孩。但后来，另一个牙买加小孩——真正的牙买加小孩——出现了，大家说：“噢，你得认识一下威尔。”

00:35:46.090 --> 00:35:55.950
<v Elna Baker>Yeah. At school, he's the Jamaican kid. But then, another Jamaican kid-- like, an actual Jamaican kid-- shows up and everyone's like, oh, you got to meet Will.
<v Elna Baker>这对他就没好事了。

00:35:55.950 --> 00:35:57.400
<v Ira Glass>Oh, that's not good for him.
<v Ira Glass>没错。

00:35:57.400 --> 00:35:58.360
<v Elna Baker>Nope.
<v Elna Baker>然后他就开始盘问我。“哪儿来的？家里哪儿的？” 我全是在编。我当时在即兴发挥。然后他停下来，说：“那家伙不是牙买加人。” 我说：“噢，该死，该死。” 然后我说：“不，不，不。你是来自牙买加，我是来自牙买加……皇后区。”

00:35:58.360 --> 00:36:24.310
<v Will>And then he just started questioning me. Oh, where you from? Where your family from? And I'm just making stuff up. I was just freestyling it. Then he paused and said, that boy not Jamaican. And I was like, oh, shit, oh, shit. And I just said, no, no, no, no. You're from Jamaica. I'm from Jamaica, Queens.
<v Will>我甚至不是来自皇后区的牙买加，我根本不是皇后区的。我来自布鲁克林，东纽约。

00:36:24.310 --> 00:36:30.600
<v Will>I'm not even from Jamaica, Queens. I'm not from Queens at all. I'm from Brooklyn, East New York.
<v Will>OK，这是被揭穿谎言的人。再讲个类别。

00:36:30.600 --> 00:36:35.290
<v Ira Glass>OK, so that's people caught in a lie. Tell me another category.
<v Ira Glass>好的，我特别喜欢那些试图避免尴尬结果反而让自己更尴尬的故事。

00:36:35.290 --> 00:36:44.490
<v Elna Baker>OK, so I especially love the category of embarrassing stories where people tried to avoid embarrassment. And in doing that, they embarrass themselves.
<v Elna Baker>OK，让我听一个那样的。

00:36:44.490 --> 00:36:46.210
<v Ira Glass>OK, let me hear one like that.
<v Ira Glass>OK，这件事发生在来自南卡罗来纳州默勒尔斯湾的玛丽·格雷女士身上。她是个伴娘，礼服寄到时发现太小了。婚礼当天，她劝说另一个穿大一号尺码的伴娘和她换礼服。礼服穿上了，但太紧了。于是她开始产生一种不理性的恐惧，觉得自己如果从礼服里挤出来会抢了新郎新娘的风头。

00:36:46.210 --> 00:37:09.280
<v Elna Baker>OK, so this happened to this lady, Mary Gray, from Murrells Inlet, South Carolina. So she's a bridesmaid whose dress arrives, and it's way too small. Day of the wedding, she convinces another bridesmaid, one size up, to swap dresses with her. This dress fits, but it's tight. And so she starts to get this irrational fear that if she's bulging out of her dress, she's going to upstage the bride and groom.
<v Elna Baker>噢，就像人们会盯着她看而不是——

00:37:09.280 --> 00:37:11.480
<v Ira Glass>Oh, like people will look at her instead of--
<v Ira Glass>没错。这绝对不行。然后她想到了一个主意：保鲜膜。

00:37:11.480 --> 00:37:17.140
<v Elna Baker>Exactly. This cannot happen. And then this idea hits her, Saran Wrap.
<v Elna Baker>所以我走进厨房，找到保鲜膜。拿出来开始裹。我一圈又一圈地裹，直到把自己勒紧，礼服顺利拉上了拉链。我想：太棒了，这简直是个天才主意。然后我就出门了。

00:37:17.140 --> 00:37:41.880
<v Mary Gray>So I go into the kitchen. I find the Saran Wrap. I get it out and I just start wrapping. And I go around and around and around until I am cinched, and the dress zips up beautifully. And I think, yeah, this is going to work. This is actually genius. And out the door I go.
<v Mary Gray>于是她在105华氏度的酷暑中站在祭坛前——

00:37:41.880 --> 00:37:45.210
<v Elna Baker>So she's standing in 105 degree heat at the altar--
<v Elna Baker>没错，南卡罗来纳。

00:37:45.210 --> 00:37:45.750
<v Ira Glass>Right, South Carolina.
<v Ira Glass>——站在新郎新娘旁边。台下有一大群人看着。她变得越来越热，越来越热。

00:37:45.750 --> 00:37:52.770
<v Elna Baker>--next to the bride and groom. And there's a big crowd watching. And she just starts getting hotter and hotter and hotter.
<v Elna Baker>我能感觉到汗水开始顺着背流下来。我看着地板，眼前出现了光斑。我是容易昏倒的人，所以我知道那意味着什么。

00:37:52.770 --> 00:38:10.480
<v Mary Gray>I can feel the sweat starting to kind of roll down my back. And I'm looking down at the floor and I'm seeing spots. And I'm a fainter, so I know what that means.
<v Mary Gray>所以，我尽可能隐蔽地转过身，请求旁边的伴郎帮我拉开拉链。当然，他完全不知道我在说什么。他说：“什么？” 我说：“我需要你拉开我的礼服拉链。” 于是他拉开了。现在所有人都知道我身上缠满了保鲜膜。

00:38:10.480 --> 00:38:38.790
<v Mary Gray>So, as subtly as possible, I turn around to my groomsman partner and I ask him to unzip my dress. And, of course, he has no idea what I'm talking about. And he's like, what? And I said, I need you to unzip my dress. So he does it. He unzips my dress. So now everyone knows that I'm wrapped up in Saran Wrap.
<v Mary Gray>你当时羞耻坏了吗？

00:38:38.790 --> 00:38:40.890
<v Elna Baker>And are you just mortified?
<v Elna Baker>我当时只是拼命想不要昏过去。所以我决定溜走。

00:38:40.890 --> 00:38:50.700
<v Mary Gray>I am just really focusing on not passing out. So I decide that I am going to take off.
<v Mary Gray>她溜掉了。礼服敞开着。她错过了仪式剩下的部分，喝了点水。

00:38:50.700 --> 00:38:55.830
<v Elna Baker>She ducks away. Her dress is open. She misses the rest of the ceremony, gets some water.
<v Elna Baker>这很有趣。她最大的恐惧是抢了新郎新娘的风头。结果她用了保鲜膜，反而导致她做了完全一样的事。她抢了新郎新娘的风头。

00:38:55.830 --> 00:39:04.190
<v Ira Glass>It's funny. Her greatest fear was that she was going to upstage the bride and groom. And so she does the Saran Wrap, and then that leads her to do exactly that. She upstages the bride and groom.
<v Ira Glass>是的，是的。她是每个人对那场婚礼记忆最深的部分。

00:39:04.190 --> 00:39:07.230
<v Elna Baker>Yeah, yeah. She is the thing that everyone remembers about that wedding.
<v Elna Baker>从头到尾，在这个故事里，我自己就是我最大的敌人。

00:39:07.230 --> 00:39:18.310
<v Mary Gray>From start to finish, I am my own worst enemy in this story.
<v Mary Gray>OK，收件箱里最让人挥之不去的故事是这个。它太尴尬了，以至于发件人不想让我们用她的真名。

00:39:18.310 --> 00:39:26.730
<v Elna Baker>OK, so the most haunting story that came into our inbox was this one. And it's so embarrassing that the woman who sent it doesn't want us to use her real name.
<v Elna Baker>OK。

00:39:26.730 --> 00:39:27.210
<v Ira Glass>OK.
<v Ira Glass>我们叫她玛雅。玛雅去年参加了GRE考试，为了申请研究生。因为疫情，她必须在家里办公时线上考试。但这过程非常严格，为了防止作弊。有监考人员通过网络摄像头盯着她。

00:39:27.210 --> 00:39:43.920
<v Elna Baker>We're going to call her Maya. So Maya is taking the GRE this last year so she could apply to grad school. And because of COVID, she had to take it online in her home office. But it's like a very regimented process, just so they know they're not cheating. There's a proctor assigned to watch her through a webcam.
<v Elna Baker>考试前，我得带他们参观一下我的考试区域。我得展示桌子底下、上面、墙壁，总之全部，确保我没作弊。我们做完了。一切就绪，我开始考试。

00:39:43.920 --> 00:40:01.860
<v Maya>So before the test, I have to give them a little tour of my testing space. I have to show under the desk, over the desk, the walls, just everything so that they know I'm not cheating. So we do that. Everything is good to go. I start the test.
<v Maya>考到一半，她胃痛。她心想：噢，不。她必须得上厕所。而且现在就要去。她努力忍到了休息时间。但令她恐惧的是，下一次休息只有1分钟。她给监考发信息，问能不能离开一会儿再回来。对方告诉她——

00:40:01.860 --> 00:40:22.330
<v Elna Baker>So partway through the test, she has this pain in her stomach. And she's like, oh, no. She's got to go. And she's got to go now. So she manages to hold it until the break. But then, to her horror, the next break is just 1 minute long, right. So she messages the proctor and she's like, can I leave and come back in a minute? And he tells her--
<v Elna Baker>如果你离开画面，你将失去考试资格。

00:40:22.330 --> 00:40:25.450
<v Maya>If you leave the frame, you will forfeit the test.
<v Maya>[倒吸凉气]

00:40:25.450 --> 00:40:26.950
<v Elna Baker>[GASP]
<v Elna Baker>我坐在那儿，心想：我不知道该怎么办。要么现在离开。放弃。去洗手间但不考GRE，放弃读研。要么做我实际做的那件事。

00:40:26.950 --> 00:40:46.890
<v Maya>And so I'm sitting there, and I'm like, I don't know what to do. Either I leave now. I give up. I go to the bathroom and don't take the GRE, give up grad school. Or I do what I actually did.
<v Maya>她实际做的是在地上解决了。

00:40:46.890 --> 00:40:50.910
<v Elna Baker>What she actually does is she does the do on the floor.
<v Elna Baker>等等，在镜头前，就像什么都没发生过一样？

00:40:50.910 --> 00:40:54.120
<v Ira Glass>Wait, while staying on camera, like nothing is happening at all?
<v Ira Glass>没错。她把手臂放在椅子上支撑身体。

00:40:54.120 --> 00:40:58.270
<v Elna Baker>Exactly. She positions her arms on the chair.
<v Elna Baker>别再描述这种心理画面了。我不想看。继续你的故事。

00:40:58.270 --> 00:41:01.880
<v Ira Glass>Stop with this mental image. I don't want to see it. Keep going with your story.
<v Ira Glass>这居然成了一个独立的类别。我们听说其他四个人在标准化考试中也面临了同样的选择。

00:41:01.880 --> 00:41:09.550
<v Elna Baker>So this is surprisingly a category unto itself. We heard from four other people who had to make this same call during a standardized test.
<v Elna Baker>什么？

00:41:09.550 --> 00:41:09.960
<v Ira Glass>What?
<v Ira Glass>嗯哼。

00:41:09.960 --> 00:41:11.440
<v Elna Baker>Mm-hmm, mm-hmm.
<v Elna Baker>也做了同样的决定？

00:41:11.440 --> 00:41:12.690
<v Ira Glass>And made the same decision?
<v Ira Glass>是的。噢，他们尝试忍住但没成功。

00:41:12.690 --> 00:41:16.470
<v Elna Baker>Yeah. Oh, they tried to hold it and it didn't work for them.
<v Elna Baker>后来呢？

00:41:16.470 --> 00:41:17.860
<v Ira Glass>So what happens?
<v Ira Glass>她以为最糟糕的过去了。她撑到了下一次休息，有10分钟。她被允许离开房间。

00:41:17.860 --> 00:41:25.330
<v Elna Baker>So she assumes the worst of it is over. She makes it all the way to the next break. It's a 10-minute break. She's allowed to leave the room.
<v Elna Baker>噢，在那次休息期间。

00:41:25.330 --> 00:41:26.110
<v Ira Glass>Oh, during that break.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:41:26.110 --> 00:41:26.530
<v Elna Baker>Yeah.
<v Elna Baker>但我很快意识到，10分钟休息结束后，我必须再次让他们检查考试区域，包括桌子底下。

00:41:26.530 --> 00:41:38.940
<v Maya>But quickly, I realized that at the end of the 10-minute break I had to once again give them this web cam tour of my testing area, including under my desk.
<v Maya>噢，天哪，不。

00:41:38.940 --> 00:41:41.260
<v Elna Baker>Oh my god, no.
<v Elna Baker>所以我意识到，OK，这10分钟休息意味着要清理干净。

00:41:41.260 --> 00:41:45.610
<v Maya>So I realize, OK, the 10-minute break means cleaning this up.
<v Maya>记住，监考还在摄像头里看着她。所以她不得不像爬行一样爬过房间。

00:41:45.610 --> 00:41:52.060
<v Elna Baker>Keep in mind, the proctor is still watching her through her webcam. So she has to army crawl across the room on the floor.
<v Elna Baker>噢，为了避开摄像头的视线。

00:41:52.060 --> 00:41:53.810
<v Ira Glass>Oh, to stay out of the camera's sight line.
<v Ira Glass>没错，没错。

00:41:53.810 --> 00:41:55.710
<v Elna Baker>Exactly, exactly.
<v Elna Baker>但你蒙混过关了，对吧？监考——

00:41:55.710 --> 00:41:58.450
<v Elna Baker>But you got away with it, right? Like, the proctor--
<v Elna Baker>嗯——

00:41:58.450 --> 00:41:58.600
<v Maya>Well--
<v Maya>——没看到，根本不知道。

00:41:58.600 --> 00:42:00.030
<v Elna Baker>--never saw, had no idea.
<v Elna Baker>事情是这样的。我在Zoom上给朋友讲了这个故事。我就坐在我参加GRE考试的那张桌子前。我讲了故事，显然，我们都在笑什么的。然后一个朋友安静地等笑声平息，说：“你背后有个镜子吗？”

00:42:00.030 --> 00:42:24.320
<v Maya>So here's the thing. I was telling this story to some friends over Zoom. And I'm sitting at the same desk where I took the GRE. And I told this story. And obviously, we're all laughing, whatever. And then one of my friends kind of like quietly waits for everything to die down and then says, is that a mirror behind you?
<v Maya>[倒吸凉气]

00:42:24.320 --> 00:42:27.090
<v Elna Baker>[GASP].
<v Elna Baker>确实有。我背后有一个全尺寸的立式镜子。于是我回到房间门口，爬进去重演了所有动作。我只听到他们在笑。我甚至都不确定，我在想：你们到底是看到了还是没看到？他们当时笑得喘不过气来。所以最终有人说：“是的，绝对看得到。”

00:42:27.090 --> 00:43:06.160
<v Maya>And it was. I have a full-sized standing mirror behind me. And so I went back to the door of the room and crawled all the way in and reenacted everything. And all I could hear was them laughing. I wasn't even sure. I was like, could you see it or not? Could you see it or not? But they, like, weren't breathing. And so, eventually, someone said, yeah, you could see everything, for sure.
<v Maya>她在这个故事里最深刻的感受是，这让她了解了关于自己的新信息。

00:43:06.160 --> 00:43:11.700
<v Elna Baker>So the thing that sticks out to her about this story is it showed her new information about herself.
<v Elna Baker>新信息是——

00:43:11.700 --> 00:43:13.510
<v Ira Glass>The new information being--
<v Ira Glass>比如，我愿意为了目标做出多卑劣的事。

00:43:13.510 --> 00:43:16.780
<v Elna Baker>Like, what lows she's willing to stoop to.
<v Elna Baker>这太有意思了，因为我认为这是一个更积极的视角，反映了她为了得到想要的东西会付出多大的努力。

00:43:16.780 --> 00:43:23.490
<v Ira Glass>That's so interesting, because I think of it in a much more positive way of how far she'll go to get what she feels like she needs to get.
<v Ira Glass>那根本不是她的感受。这就是这些尴尬故事的一个重点。人们大多数时候不会从积极的角度去想。

00:43:23.490 --> 00:43:33.530
<v Elna Baker>That is not her take on it at all. That's one of the big things with these embarrassing stories. People never take it the positive way, for the most part.
<v Elna Baker>噢。

00:43:33.530 --> 00:43:33.910
<v Ira Glass>Oh.
<v Ira Glass>他们总是会想：“我这一生都知道自己有点问题，现在我知道了。”

00:43:33.910 --> 00:43:40.360
<v Elna Baker>They always take it the way where they're like, I knew all my life something was wrong with me, and now I know.
<v Elna Baker>现在，永远地，我读研的经历都被这段体验玷污了。让我无法释怀的是，这是我的选择。它不是发生在我身上的。这是我的选择。那会让它变得更好还是更糟？我不知道。

00:43:40.360 --> 00:43:58.930
<v Maya>Now, forever more, me getting into grad school is tainted by this experience. The thing that I can't get over with this is that it was a choice I made. It didn't happen to me. It was a choice. Does that make it better or worse? I don't know.
<v Maya>我觉得这让它变得更糟。

00:43:58.930 --> 00:44:02.200
<v Elna Baker>I mean, I think it makes it worse.
<v Elna Baker>（笑）我想也是。

00:44:02.200 --> 00:44:10.280
<v Maya>(LAUGHING) I think so.
<v Maya>所以艾尔娜，你是超级对尴尬感兴趣的人。你思考了很多，也经历了很多。这一千个故事里，有什么是你以前不知道的吗？

00:44:10.280 --> 00:44:24.820
<v Ira Glass>So Elna, you're somebody who's super interested in embarrassment. Like, you've thought about it a lot. You've experienced it a lot. Is there something that going through a thousand of these stories showed you, that you didn't actually know before?
<v Ira Glass>在这些邮件里，人们被这些错误折磨着。完全感到尴尬，并且常常终身背负。有时那只是很小的错误。读着它们，我心想：噢，其实问题在于我们认为自己不该犯错。我们认为自己必须在这一生中做到不犯错。而如果你说，好吧，在我这一生中，我会在公共场合摔倒，我的衣服会在不该露的时候露出来……你必须接受这是过程的一部分，是交易的一部分。

00:44:24.820 --> 00:45:07.480
<v Elna Baker>People in these emails, they were tortured by these mistakes. Totally embarrassed and often carried them their whole lives. And sometimes they were really small mistakes. And reading them, I feel like it just made me think, like, oh, actually, I think the problem is that we think we're not supposed to make mistakes. We think we're supposed to make it through this life without doing this, that, or the other. And if you just say, you know what, over the course of my lifetime, I'm going to trip and fall in public, my top is going to fly off when it's not supposed to. You just have to accept that that is part of it, that's part of the deal.
<v Elna Baker>我喜欢你给节目带来的这个暖心的结局。

00:45:07.480 --> 00:45:10.350
<v Ira Glass>I like that you're bringing this to a heartwarming ending.
<v Ira Glass>我知道你在挖苦我，但不用谢。

00:45:10.350 --> 00:45:27.280
<v Elna Baker>I know that you're being sarcastic, but you're welcome.
<v Elna Baker>我想分享最后一个尴尬故事。第四幕，“把尴尬赤裸裸地呈现出来”。

00:45:27.280 --> 00:45:33.640
<v Elna Baker>I want to share one last embarrassing story. Act 4, Putting the Bare Ass in Embarrassing.
<v Elna Baker>在你寄来的所有尴尬故事中，最常见的一种情况是你有点裸体，或者全身裸体，出现在公共场合。这是一个常见的恐惧。最频繁的噩梦之一就是在梦中低头发现自己在别人面前裸体。

00:45:33.640 --> 00:45:51.490
<v Elna Baker>Of all the kinds of embarrassing stories you sent in, one of the most common ones was a situation where you were a little bit naked or all the way naked, in public. This is a common fear. One of the most frequent nightmares is the dream where you look down and realize you're naked in front of other people.
<v Elna Baker>弗洛伊德甚至在《梦的解析》中写过这一点。他称之为“裸露的尴尬梦”，引用说：“我相信我的绝大多数读者在梦中都曾处于过这种境地。” 当我们在清醒生活中感到尴尬时，我们经常会做这种梦。

00:45:51.490 --> 00:46:11.340
<v Elna Baker>Freud even wrote about it in The Interpretation of Dreams. He called it The Embarrassment Dream of Nakedness, and said, quote, "I believe that the great majority of my readers will at some time have found themselves in this situation in a dream." When we feel embarrassed in our waking life, we often have this dream.
<v Elna Baker>下一位讲述者，来自纳什维尔，肯定做过这个梦。她叫卡里亚德。她个人对在公共场合裸体一点兴趣都没有。事实上，即使是泳装对她来说也是勉强。

00:46:11.340 --> 00:46:24.000
<v Elna Baker>The person in this next story, who wrote in from Nashville, has definitely had this dream. Her name is Cariad. She personally has no interest in being naked in public. In fact, even bathing suits are a stretch for her.
<v Elna Baker>我觉得办泳池派对的人都是彻头彻尾的心理变态。我不明白为什么要那样做。你知道吗？我从小就这么想。幸运的是，英国没那么多这种派对。但我过去常来这里探望祖母，人们会举办泳池派对。我会问：为什么要这样？我现在还是这么想。

00:46:24.000 --> 00:46:43.230
<v Cariad>I think people who have pool parties are total psychopaths. I don't understand why you would do that. You know? I have since I was a kid. Luckily, there aren't a lot in England. But I used to come here to visit my grandmother, and people would throw pool parties. And I'd be like, why? And I still feel that way.
<v Cariad>你为什么对我们所有人做这种事？

00:46:43.230 --> 00:46:45.010
<v Elna Baker>Why are you doing this to all of us?
<v Elna Baker>是的，我才不想为了你的生日去什么该死的海滩。

00:46:45.010 --> 00:46:54.810
<v Cariad>Yeah, I don't want to go to the fucking beach for your goddamn birthday.
<v Cariad>你马上要听到的故事发生于几年前，卡里亚德当时住在纽约市。她刚开始和一个她很喜欢的男人约会，一个急诊室医生，性格开朗，总是微笑，可靠。他讲了很多关于急诊室的有趣故事。我们叫他道格。

00:46:54.810 --> 00:47:12.960
<v Elna Baker>The story you're about to hear happened years ago, when Cariad was living in New York City. She had just started seeing this guy she liked a lot, an emergency room doctor, big personality, always smiling, reliable. He told a lot of funny stories about the ER. We're calling him Doug.
<v Elna Baker>根据她的记忆，他们已经约会一个月左右了。他们曾在她家过夜，这是第一次在她家以外的地方过夜。当卡里亚德昏昏欲睡时——

00:47:12.960 --> 00:47:23.540
<v Elna Baker>The way she remembers it, they'd been seeing each other for about a month. They'd slept over at her place, and it's the first night she stays over at his. As Cariad is drifting off to sleep--
<v Elna Baker>我只记得非常、非常累，非常想上厕所。那是我想睡着前的最后一个念头，心想：天呐，我真得去尿尿，但我懒得起床找厕所。然后我做了个梦，梦见我真的需要去厕所。我找啊找，到处都找不到。我推着一扇大金属门。那是锁着的。我过不去，我感到非常、非常沮丧。

00:47:23.540 --> 00:47:54.960
<v Cariad>I just remember being really, really tired and really needing to go to the bathroom. And that was kind of my last thought as I was falling to sleep was, ugh, I really need to pee, but I can't be bothered to get up and find the bathroom. And then I had this dream that I really needed to go to the bathroom. And I was looking for one and I couldn't find one anywhere. And I was pushing this big metal door, in my search for the bathroom. And it was locked. And I couldn't get through, and I was really, really frustrated.
<v Cariad>然后，我醒了。我光着身子站在楼梯间，推着一扇从楼梯间通往公寓楼其他部分的门。

00:47:54.960 --> 00:48:13.520
<v Cariad>And then, I woke up. And I was naked, standing in a stairwell, pushing against a big door that went from the stairwell into the rest of the apartment building.
<v Cariad>噢，天哪。

00:48:13.520 --> 00:48:16.640
<v Elna Baker>Oh my god.
<v Elna Baker>感觉就像大脑融化了。这种情况完全不合逻辑。

00:48:16.640 --> 00:48:26.330
<v Cariad>It was like my mind melted. The logic of the situation made no sense.
<v Cariad>卡里亚德梦游走出了道格的公寓。显然，她小时候有过几次这种情况，虽然她完全不记得了。而且她成年后从没梦游过。所以她没想到发生了什么。

00:48:26.330 --> 00:48:40.800
<v Elna Baker>Cariad had sleepwalked out of Doug's apartment. Apparently, she'd done this as a small child, just a few times, though she had no memory of it at all. And she'd never sleepwalked as an adult. So it didn't occur to her that that's what's happened.
<v Elna Baker>我觉得自己彻底疯了。我就想：我怎么在这里？这怎么可能发生？我怎么醒着的？

00:48:40.800 --> 00:48:57.090
<v Cariad>I felt like I completely lost my mind. Like, just, how am I here? How is this a thing that is happening? How am I awake?
<v Cariad>卡里亚德推开楼梯间的金属门，如释重负地看到走廊尽头是道格的公寓。她跑了过去。

00:48:57.090 --> 00:49:05.420
<v Elna Baker>Cariad pushes open the metal stairwell door and sees, to her relief, Doug's apartment at the end of the hall. She runs towards it.
<v Elna Baker>然后我意识到，我不知道这到底是不是他的公寓。我转过身看，这就是纽约那种公寓楼，每层楼的公寓看起来都一模一样。我意识到我不能——

00:49:05.420 --> 00:49:19.400
<v Cariad>And then I realized, like, I don't know if this is his apartment. And I turned around and looked, and it was just one of those New York buildings, oh, just a bunch of apartments to each floor that all kind of looked the same. And I realized I can't--
<v Cariad>这感觉像场噩梦。

00:49:19.400 --> 00:49:21.280
<v Elna Baker>This feels like a nightmare.
<v Elna Baker>这是一场绝对的噩梦。

00:49:21.280 --> 00:49:25.080
<v Cariad>This was an absolute fucking nightmare.
<v Cariad>卡里亚德靠近她认为是道格家门的公寓，虽然她不确定。

00:49:25.080 --> 00:49:29.720
<v Elna Baker>Cariad approaches the door she thinks his Doug's apartment, though she's not sure.
<v Elna Baker>所以我决定敲敲门。然后在他们回应前，我跑回楼梯间。我把耳朵贴在门上。我把楼梯间的门打开一点缝以便听清。我看不见他们，他们也看不见我。这样如果声音是我认识的，我就能跑过去进公寓。如果是不认识的，他们就看不到我。希望他们能关上门。

00:49:29.720 --> 00:50:00.070
<v Cariad>So what I decide to do is knock on the door. And then I run away from the door, back into the stairwell, before they answer. And I put my ear to the door. I crack the door to the stairwell so I can hear them. But I can't see them, they can't see me. This way, if it's a voice I recognize, I can run around the corner and into the apartment. And if it's a voice I don't recognize, they won't see me. And hopefully they'll just close the door.
<v Cariad>这计划相当聪明。

00:50:00.070 --> 00:50:02.710
<v Elna Baker>That's a pretty brilliant plan.
<v Elna Baker>谢谢，谢谢。我照做了，那声音我不认识。我开始意识到我所处局势的恐怖程度。人们要起床去上班了，那发生时我不能在那儿。顺便说一句，我还是很想尿尿。我尿急到甚至因此醒来并梦游了。

00:50:02.710 --> 00:50:38.890
<v Cariad>Thank you, thank you. So I do that, and it is not a voice I recognize. And I start to realize the depth of the horror of the situation that I'm in. People are going to start getting up and going to work, and I cannot be there when that happens. By the way, I still really need to pee. Like, I need to pee so badly that it woke me up and made me sleepwalk, you know?
<v Cariad>她决定光着身子尿尿最隐蔽的地方是楼梯间底部，靠着去大厅的门。如果有人想开门，她能顶回去。如果有人从楼梯下来，她能听到。所以她吞下尊严，真的那么做了。

00:50:38.890 --> 00:50:55.340
<v Elna Baker>She decides the least conspicuous place to pee while naked is at the bottom of the stairwell, leaning against the door to the lobby. If someone tries to open the door, she can push back. And if someone's coming down the stairs, she'll hear them. So she swallows her pride and does it.
<v Elna Baker>我真是太恐怖了。我简直恐怖透了。我记得非常清楚自己坐在楼梯上哭。这种情况太荒谬了，我没法摆脱它。如果我当时多留意一下环境，就会知道他在2B室或者什么的。天呐，我在电梯里怎么就没留心呢？

00:50:55.340 --> 00:51:28.330
<v Cariad>I was just so horrified. I was so horrified. I remember very clearly just sitting down on this stairwell and just crying. This was just such a ridiculous situation that I couldn't get myself out of. And if only I had been more aware of my surroundings, I would have known that he was in apartment 2B, or whatever it was. Like, how could I have not paid attention in the lift?
<v Cariad>我记得擦干眼泪，甚至没法用袖子擦鼻涕。我只能用手臂擦，心想：好吧，振作点。你必须掌控局面并解决它。你没得选。你必须解决它。所以我开始非常轻柔地尝试公寓楼所有的门。

00:51:28.330 --> 00:52:05.440
<v Cariad>And I remember drying my eyes and, like, I couldn't even wipe my nose on my sleeve. I'm wiping my nose on my arm and just thinking, all right, buck up. You have to take control of this situation and fix it. You do not have an option. You just have to fix it. And so I started very gently trying all of the doors in the apartment building.
<v Cariad>她希望她出来的那个门还没锁。

00:52:05.440 --> 00:52:09.310
<v Elna Baker>Her hope is, whatever door she came out of will still be unlocked.
<v Elna Baker>我系统地一层层试，每一个门都试了。在顶楼，有一户公寓能听到说话声。他们玩得很开心，听起来像是在开派对。

00:52:09.310 --> 00:52:25.150
<v Cariad>And I went systematically floor to floor and I tried every door. And on the top floor there was one apartment that I could hear voices in. And they were having a great time. It sounded like someone was having a party.
<v Cariad>如果没别的办法，她想，我就敲这个门。但首先，她又试了所有的门。然后答案出现了——邮箱。他的名字会在邮箱上。她跑向一楼，透过门看到大厅全是玻璃窗。没有办法走到邮箱那边，而不被街上所有人看到她裸体。

00:52:25.150 --> 00:52:50.650
<v Elna Baker>If she can't find another solution, she thinks, I'm going to knock on this door. But first, she tries all the doors again. And then the answer occurs to her-- mailboxes. His name will be on his mailbox. She runs to the ground floor and sees through the door that the lobby is all glass windows. There's no way to get to the mailboxes without everyone in the street seeing her naked.
<v Elna Baker>所以她又跑回顶楼那个派对，按了门铃，然后躲在门后。一个女人出来。显然喝醉了。卡里亚德叫住她，问能不能借口衣服，对方照做了。

00:52:50.650 --> 00:53:06.430
<v Elna Baker>So she heads back up to that party on the top floor, rings the bell, and then runs and hides behind the door. A woman comes out. She's clearly tipsy. Cariad calls to her, asking if she can come over to the stairwell, which the woman does.
<v Elna Baker>你甚至怎么能遮住或藏住自己？

00:53:06.430 --> 00:53:09.850
<v Elna Baker>How are you even able to, like, cover or hide yourself?
<v Elna Baker>我是说，真的没法……就像漫画里那样。一只手臂遮住胸部，小手像个无花果叶。在这种情况下，你只能这样。

00:53:09.850 --> 00:53:24.700
<v Cariad>I mean, there really is no-- it's just that cartoon. One arm across your boobs and your little hand is a fig leaf. I mean, that's all you can do in that situation.
<v Cariad>卡里亚德解释了窘境并询问能否借件衣服。那个女人看起来完全没受影响。好像这种事经常发生一样。她示意卡里亚德进来然后走回派对。卡里亚德想：等等，不行，不不不。但门要关了，她只能勉强跟着进去。五个人在那儿。他们看到了她裸体。卡里亚德钻到沙发后面。女人给她拿了一件运动服。

00:53:24.700 --> 00:53:50.040
<v Elna Baker>Cariad explains her predicament and asks if she can borrow some clothes. The woman seems totally unfazed. Like, of course, this happens all the time. She gestures for Cariad to come on in and walks back into the party. Cariad's like, wait, no, no, no. But the door is closing, so she reluctantly follows. Five people are there. They see her naked. Cariad dives behind a couch. The woman brings her a tracksuit.
<v Elna Baker>整个派对的人都动员起来帮卡里亚德解决这个情况。她向他们描述了道格。女人觉得她知道他住哪儿。他们敲了一两扇门，叫醒了并非他的邻居。他们给卡里亚德手机打电话，没人接。最终，女人让卡里亚德直接在沙发上睡。卡里亚德第二天早上7点醒来，再次拨打手机，终于接通了。

00:53:50.040 --> 00:54:22.260
<v Elna Baker>The whole party rallies to try and help Cariad solve this situation. She describes Doug to them. The woman thinks she knows where he lives. They knock on one or two doors, waking up neighbors who are not him. They call Cariad's cell, no answer. Eventually, the woman tells Cariad to just sleep on the couch. Cariad wakes up at 7:00 the next morning, tries her phone again, and gets an answer.
<v Elna Baker>电话那头的人不是和我在一起的那个男人。是别人。而且他们很生气。他一直说：“你叫什么？你叫什么？” 我一直在说：“我要和那个男的说话。” 他们不肯把电话给他。

00:54:22.260 --> 00:54:36.780
<v Cariad>And the person on the phone is not the guy that I had been with. It's somebody else. And they're really angry. And he kept saying, what's your name, what's your name. And I kept saying, I want to speak to this guy. And they wouldn't hand the phone over.
<v Cariad>我变得很生气，因为我在想：谁啊？把电话给他。他们说：“你叫什么名字？” 我说卡里亚德。然后他们说：“你姓什么？” 我说哈蒙。然后那男人接了电话。

00:54:36.780 --> 00:54:49.650
<v Cariad>And I was getting really pissed because I was like, well, who are you? Just give him the phone. And they said, what's your name, what's your name? And I said Cariad. And then they said, what's your last name? And I said Harmon. And then this guy gets on the phone.
<v Cariad>她指的是道格。

00:54:49.650 --> 00:54:50.940
<v Elna Baker>She means Doug.
<v Elna Baker>他只是说：“搞什么鬼？你们在哪儿……”他听起来很生气。

00:54:50.940 --> 00:54:57.840
<v Cariad>And he's just like, what the fuck? Where are-- he's, like, really angry.
<v Cariad>原来，在卡里亚德光着身子在走廊跑的时候，他半夜醒来发现卡里亚德的衣服、钱包、手机都在，人却没了。就像消失了一样。

00:54:57.840 --> 00:55:11.050
<v Elna Baker>It turns out, while Cariad been running around the hallways naked, he'd woken up in the middle of the night to find Cariad's clothes, wallet, phone, but no Cariad. It was like she'd vanished.
<v Elna Baker>我和道格谈过了。他不想上电台，但他说了这些。他到处找她。他看了室友房间，看她是不是不小心爬进了他们床上。然后他打开冰箱，又打开烤箱，然后才反应过来：她为什么会在烤箱里？

00:55:11.050 --> 00:55:28.670
<v Elna Baker>I talked to Doug. He didn't want to be on the radio, but here's what he said. He looked for her everywhere. He peeked into his roommate's room to see if maybe she'd accidentally crawled in their bed. Then he opened the fridge, then the oven, before catching himself and thinking, why would she be in the oven?
<v Elna Baker>接下来，他搜了整栋楼，楼外。最终，他打了她朋友电话。他们不知道该怎么办。他想：她失踪太久了，还没穿衣服。这可能很严重。这时快天亮了，所以我们打了报警电话。警察20分钟后到了。他解释说：“我和这女孩出去的。她回到这，似乎迷路了。”

00:55:28.670 --> 00:55:59.090
<v Elna Baker>Next, he searched the building, outside the building. Eventually, he called one of her friends. They didn't know what to do. He thought, she's been gone long enough with no clothes on. This could be serious. By now, it was almost morning, so we called the police. They showed up 20 minutes later. He explained to them, I was out with this girl. She came back here. She appears to be lost.
<v Elna Baker>其中一个警察开始咄咄逼人地盘问他。他不确定他们是不是就是混蛋，但他记得当时想他们可能是因为他是黑人而更怀疑他。情况瞬间升级。正如道格说的：“我记得当时感觉这是我处过最糟的情况。这家伙根本不信我说的是实话。也许他以为我杀了她。” 就在局势升级时，卡里亚德终于打通了电话。

00:55:59.090 --> 00:56:32.170
<v Elna Baker>One of the officers started questioning him aggressively. He's not certain if they were just jerks, but he remembers thinking they were probably more suspicious of him because he was Black. It went from 0 to 100 fast. As Doug put it, I remember feeling like this is the most screwed-up situation I've ever been in. This dude doesn't believe I'm telling the truth. Maybe he thinks I killed her. Just as things were escalating, Cariad finally got through on the phone.
<v Elna Baker>卡里亚德乘电梯下到道格那层。当她走出时，警察迎上来。她意识到这就是接电话的人。解释清楚后，警察走了，只剩下卡里亚德和道格。

00:56:32.170 --> 00:56:45.400
<v Elna Baker>Cariad takes the elevator down to Doug's floor. When she steps out, the police greet her. She realizes this is who'd answered the phone. Once she explains her side of the story, they leave, and it's just Cariad and Doug.
<v Elna Baker>我想我花了一分钟才认识到这对他的严重性。但我想一旦我真正意识到他在那一晚的某个时刻曾是一个嫌疑人——噢，他们根本没站在他那一边。

00:56:45.400 --> 00:57:06.550
<v Cariad>I think it took me a minute to recognize the gravity of what had happened to him. But I think once I really realized that at one point in the evening he was a suspect, like, oh, they were not on your side.
<v Cariad>真有趣。这太像是白人会有的那种想法了。

00:57:06.550 --> 00:57:10.080
<v Elna Baker>That's funny. That's such a white person thing to think.
<v Elna Baker>是的，是的，当然。你知道，在那一刻，我感受到了差距。我感受到了那一刻我们对彼此是多么不了解。但我记得我一直在说：“对不起，对不起。” 因为他确实很受惊。而他，作为一个可爱的人，只是说：“我……我很高兴你没事。但我得去过我的一天了。”

00:57:10.080 --> 00:57:49.950
<v Cariad>Yeah, yeah, of course. Of course it is, yeah. You know, I felt the gap. I felt how much we didn't really know each other, in that moment. But I just remember saying I'm so sorry, I'm so sorry. Because he was genuinely very shaken up. And he, being the lovely guy that he was, he was just like, I'm just-- I'm glad that you're OK. But I have to get on with my day.
<v Cariad>他们再没约会过，不过主要是因为他几天后搬到了全国的另一边。这次约会本计划作为最后在一起的夜晚。但他们保持了联系。一年后，他回来探访，他和卡里亚德与他的朋友们去吃晚饭。

00:57:49.950 --> 00:58:05.880
<v Elna Baker>They never went out again, though mostly because he moved across the country a few days later. This date had been planned as kind of a last night together. But they stayed in touch. A year later, when he came back to visit, he and Cariad went to dinner with his friends.
<v Elna Baker>身为绅士，我记得我们正在聊天，他说：“噢，我还没告诉他们那个故事。” 我说：“噢，没关系。如果你想讲，你可以讲。” 他说：“不不不不不。” 我想他是担心让我尴尬。

00:58:05.880 --> 00:58:18.550
<v Cariad>The gentleman that he is, I remember we were sort of chatting, and he said, oh, I haven't told them about the story. And I said, oh, it's fine. If you want to tell them, you could tell them. He said, no, no, no, no, no. I think he was worried about embarrassing me.
<v Cariad>然后我去洗手间回来，大家都在哄笑。他看着我说：“我讲了。我讲了。我忍不住。” 我们都笑了。他还省略了那一部分，为了让故事好笑。

00:58:18.550 --> 00:58:34.190
<v Cariad>And then I went to the bathroom and I came back, and everybody was cracking up. And he looked at me and he said, I told them. I told them. I couldn't help it. And we all laughed about it. He also left that part out, in order for it to be funny.
<v Cariad>既然事情最终结局还可以，他们现在在派对上都这么讲。他们省略了恐惧。

00:58:34.190 --> 00:58:41.640
<v Elna Baker>Since things came out OK in the end, they each tell it this way at parties. They leave out the fear.
<v Elna Baker>所以，本周节目中我听到和收集到的大部分尴尬故事，当事人做了一些导致自己尴尬的事情。比如那位把保鲜膜裹在身上的女士。或者在拍卖会上举牌搞砸的人。

00:58:41.640 --> 00:58:59.740
<v Elna Baker>So, most of the embarrassing stories I've heard and collected for this week's show, the person does something that results in their embarrassment. Like, we have this lady, she Saran Wraps herself for a wedding. There's someone who raises a paddle in an auction and it goes awry.
<v Elna Baker>所以我明白他们为什么感到尴尬。但你什么都没做。你没越界。你没犯错，但你依然感到尴尬。

00:58:59.740 --> 00:59:12.200
<v Elna Baker>And so, I get why they feel embarrassed. But you didn't do anything. You didn't overstep. You didn't make a mistake, but you still feel embarrassed.
<v Elna Baker>是的。我想如果你从这事里去掉责任，也许那种尴尬的味道就少了一点。但它并没有消除尴尬。光着身子跑进一堆陌生人的房间里，这本身就是尴尬的。

00:59:12.200 --> 00:59:31.620
<v Cariad>Yeah. I suppose if you take blame off the table, it takes maybe a certain flavor of that embarrassment out. But it doesn't take the embarrassment away. There is something inherently embarrassing about running into a room full of people you don't know, naked.
<v Cariad>就像，那件事确实发生在你身上。

00:59:31.620 --> 00:59:33.940
<v Elna Baker>Like, it still happened to you.
<v Elna Baker>它确实发生了，是啊。我想我现在应该感到“防弹”了，不是吗？我想一旦发生了如此尴尬的事情，你会以某种方式跨过那个门槛。如果那都没事，那为什么还要感到尴尬？长久以来，我都在想：“卡里亚德，你让自己走出了那个困境。你不在下东区的楼梯间里赤身裸体。你能应付得了。”

00:59:33.940 --> 01:00:19.720
<v Cariad>It still happened to me, yeah. I suppose I should feel pretty bulletproof now, shouldn't I? I think once something really so embarrassing happens, I do think you sort of cross that threshold somehow. And if that's OK, then why be embarrassed? And for a long time, I would think, Cariad, you got yourself out of this situation. You're not naked in a stairwell on the Lower East Side. You can figure this out.
<v Cariad>我喜欢节目在这个地方结束，不仅是因为那个人感到尴尬。她经历了一个如同尴尬隐喻的体验，一个我们大多数人只在噩梦中知晓的经历，并从中变得更好。我喜欢认为这正是尴尬所能做到的。

01:00:19.720 --> 01:00:57.710
<v Elna Baker>I like ending the show there, someone who wasn't just embarrassed. She lived the experience that's like a metaphor for embarrassment, an experience most of us only know from nightmares, and came out better for it. I like thinking that's what embarrassment can do.
